      {
        "heading": "The NIST Incident Response Lifecycle: From Preparation to Lessons Learned",
        "content": "You already know NIST SP 800-61 defines the words event, alert, and incident. The same document — reissued as **Revision 3 in April 2025**, realigned to the NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 — also defines the four-phase lifecycle that governs everything a SOC does once an incident is declared. This is the process the whole earlier lesson has been building toward: what happens after your ticket says 'malicious'.\n\n### The four phases\n\n**Preparation** happens before anything goes wrong: building detection rules, writing playbooks, running tabletop exercises, and making sure logging and access controls exist in the first place. Most of the tools and training in this course exist because of this phase — you cannot detect what you never learned to log. **Detection and Analysis** is the phase you have been living in throughout this lesson: noticing symptoms, triaging alerts, and confirming whether an incident is genuinely underway — this is where your five-question framework and your benign/suspicious/malicious verdict actually operate.\n\n**Containment, Eradication, and Recovery** is one combined phase in the NIST model, covering three related but distinct actions: **containment** stops the bleeding (isolating a host, disabling an account) without necessarily undoing the root cause yet; **eradication** removes the actual cause (deleting malware, closing the vulnerability that let the attacker in); **recovery** restores affected systems to normal operation and verifies they are clean before reconnecting them. Finally, **Post-Incident Activity** — often called the 'lessons learned' phase — reviews what happened, updates playbooks and detections based on the gaps found, and feeds directly back into Preparation for the next incident. NIST explicitly designs this as a **cycle**, not a straight line: every incident should make the next Preparation phase stronger.\n\n| Phase | What happens | Where this lesson's skills fit |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Preparation | Build detections, playbooks, logging, training | The foundation everything else in your training rests on |\n| Detection & Analysis | Notice symptoms, triage, confirm the incident | The five-question framework, the benign/suspicious/malicious verdict |\n| Containment, Eradication & Recovery | Stop it, remove the cause, restore normal operation | Escalation and the actions recommended in your ticket |\n| Post-Incident Activity | Review, learn, update detections and playbooks | Feeds back into Preparation — the cycle repeats |\n\n### Why Tier 1 needs to know all four phases\n\nA Tier 1 analyst spends most of a shift inside Detection and Analysis, but understanding the full cycle changes how you write a ticket. A recommendation like 'isolate the host' is a **containment** action, distinct from 'reimage the machine and rotate credentials', which is **eradication**, and distinct again from 'confirm the host is clean and restore its network access', which is **recovery**. Labeling your recommended actions by phase makes your escalation immediately actionable for whoever picks it up next, and it is exactly the vocabulary Tier 2/3 and incident commanders use to coordinate a real response.",
        "codeExample": "// Mapping the earlier ransomware alert (#48219, FIN-PC7) onto the NIST 800-61 lifecycle\n\n// PREPARATION (already done before this shift):\n//   - EDR deployed on FIN-PC7, ransomware-behavior detection rule exists\n//   - Playbook \"Ransomware Response\" pre-written and rehearsed\n\n// DETECTION & ANALYSIS (this is the triage you already did):\n//   ALERT #48219 \"EDR: ransomware behavior on FIN-PC7\" -> verdict: MALICIOUS\n\n// CONTAINMENT, ERADICATION & RECOVERY (three distinct actions - label them separately):\n//   CONTAIN:   isolate FIN-PC7 from the network NOW (stop it spreading)\n//   ERADICATE: remove the malicious binary, patch the entry vulnerability\n//   RECOVER:   restore FIN-PC7 from clean backup, verify no reinfection, reconnect\n\n// POST-INCIDENT ACTIVITY (days later):\n//   - Why did EDR alert 4 minutes AFTER encryption started, not before?\n//   - Tune the detection rule to fire on early-stage indicators\n//   - Feed the new detection back into PREPARATION for next time"
      },
      {
        "heading": "MITRE ATT&CK, the Pyramid of Pain, and How Threat Intelligence Actually Prioritizes Work",
        "content": "You have already asked 'is this normal for this entity' and 'what fired the rule' on every alert. Two more frameworks sit behind those questions in any mature SOC: a shared vocabulary for describing what an attacker actually did, and a model for deciding which evidence is worth chasing at all.\n\n### MITRE ATT&CK: a shared language for attacker behavior\n\n**MITRE ATT&CK** is a public, continuously updated knowledge base that catalogs real-world adversary behavior. Its Enterprise matrix organizes attacker behavior into **14 tactics** — columns representing the attacker's *goal* at each stage, such as Initial Access, Execution, Persistence, Privilege Escalation, Lateral Movement, and Exfiltration. Under each tactic sit dozens of **techniques** (and finer **sub-techniques**) — the *how*: the specific method used to achieve that goal. Every technique has a stable ID like `T1110` (Brute Force) or `T1046` (Network Service Discovery, the port-scanning technique from the networking lesson). A **procedure** is the concrete, real-world instance of a technique — the exact tool and command a specific attacker group actually used. Tactics answer *why*, techniques answer *how*, procedures answer *what exactly happened this time* — together these three make up **TTPs**.\n\nWhy this matters day to day: when you write 'brute-force login followed by success' in a ticket, tagging it `T1110` instantly tells every other analyst, every detection engineer, and every future report reader exactly what class of behavior you mean, in a vocabulary the entire industry shares — no ambiguity, no re-explaining.\n\n### IOCs vs IOAs\n\nAn **IOC** (Indicator of Compromise) is forensic evidence that an attack already happened — a specific bad hash, IP, or domain, an after-the-fact artifact. An **IOA** (Indicator of Attack) is evidence of intent and technique *while it is happening* — a behavior pattern, like a process spawning a shell, that reveals an attack in progress regardless of which specific tool is used. IOCs are what you match against; IOAs are what a rule based on ATT&CK behavior actually catches.\n\n### The Pyramid of Pain: not all evidence is equally useful\n\nSecurity researcher David Bianco's **Pyramid of Pain** (2013) ranks the six kinds of indicators you might use to detect or block an attacker, from the bottom (trivial for the attacker to change) to the top (painful for them to change):\n\n1. **Hash values** — trivial: one changed byte produces a totally different hash.\n2. **IP addresses** — easy: attackers rotate infrastructure constantly.\n3. **Domain names** — simple but a bit more friction than an IP to re-register.\n4. **Network / host artifacts** — annoying: things like a distinctive registry key or user-agent string.\n5. **Tools** — challenging: forces the attacker to retool or find a new utility entirely.\n6. **TTPs** — tough: this is the attacker's actual tradecraft; blocking it forces them to redesign their whole operation, which is expensive.\n\nThe insight for a SOC: a detection rule built only on hashes or IPs (the bottom of the pyramid) gives short-lived value, because attackers rotate that infrastructure in minutes. A detection built on TTPs — the behavior itself, expressed as ATT&CK techniques — imposes real cost on the attacker and survives infrastructure changes. This is exactly why mature SOCs invest in **behavioral** detections (UEBA, EDR process-behavior rules) over simple **IOC-matching** rules, even though IOC feeds are cheap and easy to consume.\n\n| Pyramid level | Example | Cost to the attacker to change |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| TTPs (top) | \"Uses Kerberoasting for credential access\" | Very high — must redesign tradecraft |\n| Tools | A specific off-the-shelf malware builder | High — must find/build a new tool |\n| Network/host artifacts | A distinctive registry key or mutex | Medium — annoying but doable |\n| Domain names | `upd4t3-cdn.net` | Low-medium — re-register a new one |\n| IP addresses | `91.203.14.6` | Low — rent new infrastructure |\n| Hashes (bottom) | A specific file's SHA-256 | Trivial — recompile changes it instantly |",
        "codeExample": "// Same brute-force incident, described three ways - notice the pyramid climbing\n\n// LOW-VALUE detection (bottom of the pyramid - IOC matching):\n//   rule: \"block src IP 91.203.14.6\"\n//   -> attacker rents a new VPS tomorrow; rule is now worthless\n\n// HIGH-VALUE detection (top of the pyramid - TTP-based):\n//   ATT&CK technique: T1110 (Brute Force)\n//   rule: \"5+ EventID 4625 for the SAME TargetUserName from ONE source,\n//          followed by a 4624 success, within 10 minutes\"\n//   -> catches this technique regardless of WHICH IP or tool the attacker\n//      uses next time. Forces them to change their whole approach.\n\n// Ticket tagging convention used across the SOC industry:\nTICKET #48211  ATT&CK: T1110 (Brute Force) -> T1078 (Valid Accounts)\n// Tactic: Credential Access -> Initial Access / Persistence\n// Any analyst worldwide reading these two IDs knows exactly what occurred."
      }
